# EXAMPLE crew script: four GPCs in a REDUNDANT SET, the way the vehicle
# actually flies one -- memory configuration 3, GNC OPS 301 (entry).  Run from
# discretePanel/:
#
#   python3 simulatePASS.py --gpcs 1-4 --crts 3 \
#       --tape ~/workspace/pass-run/OI340700-v44boot.mmv \
#       --script examples/4gpc-g3-startup.script --duration 1200
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT 4gpc-startup.script.  That one asks for OPS 201, and OPS 2
# ON ORBIT IS A TWO-GPC SET: the flight MEMORY CONFIGURATION TABLE gives
# CONFIG 2 a GPC SEL of 1,2 with strings 1,3 to one computer and 2,4 to the
# other, and the post-insertion procedure then HALTs the rest.  Four computers
# in one set is CONFIG 1 (ascent) or CONFIG 3 (entry), not CONFIG 2.  The older
# script asks for MC 2 while assigning buses the CONFIG 3 way, which is a
# mixture that never flies.  This script is CONFIG 3 throughout.
#
# The table, from STS-134 Post Insertion (local mirror, Shuttle/Post Insertion,
# book page 1-3), CONFIG 3 column:
#     GPC SEL 1,2,3,4    STR 1->1 2->2 3->3 4->4    P/L 0
#     CRT 1->1 2->2 3->3 4->0        L 1->0 2->0    MM 1->1 2->2
#
# THREE CRTs, AND THE THIRD IS POWERED LAST -- which is the whole trick.
# CONFIG 3 gives CRT3 to GPC3, and it is worth having: a computer that drops
# out of the set still has a keyboard into it, which every recovery attempt on
# 2026-09-16 lacked.
#
# But a display powered before it has been ASSIGNED goes to whoever is already
# running.  USA005350 3.2.15.1: a PASS GPC moded to RUN takes IDPs 1 TO 3 when
# the common set commands no DK buses -- so with all three powered during the
# IPLs, GPC1 alone drives every display while the others idle.  ONE GPC DRIVING
# THREE DISPLAYS NEEDS MORE THAN ONE CORE, and a GPC only gets one: measured,
# the vehicle then runs at rate 0.42 instead of 1.00, the barrier paces every
# computer to the slowest, MEDS2's wall-clock watchdogs start firing, and
# whichever computer is unlucky fails itself out.  Three runs lost GPC1+GPC2,
# then GPC2, then GPC3.
#
# Powering IDP3 only after the set is formed and ITEM 14 has given CRT3 to GPC3
# means no computer ever drives more than one display.  Three displays then
# cost nothing at all -- rate stays 1.00.  See ledger #159.
#
# Each IPL is ipl-one-gpc.script, unchanged -- the IPL procedure does not care
# which OPS follows it.
#
# VERIFIED 2026-09-17, unattended: a FULL FOUR-COMPUTER REDUNDANT SET in
# OPS 301, every computer driving its own flight-critical strings (bus 14/20
# GPC1, 15/21 GPC2, 16/22 GPC3, 17/23 GPC4).  With two CRTs it ran 36,319 /
# 36,322 / 36,320 / 36,308 sync issues per minute, matched to within 30.  With
# the third CRT powered late, as below, all four hold at rate 1.00 (SSIP cadence
# 160.04 / 160.05 / 160.08 / 160.04 ms against a nominal 160) and CRT3 takes its
# fills with no bus timeouts at all -- where powering it early cost 120.

# --- all four GPCs to HALT, the IDPs on ---------------------------------------
+0     gpc 1
+0     mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 2
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 3
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.01  gpc 4
+0.01  mode HALT
+0.02  idppower 1 on
+0.01  idppower 2 on

# --- each computer in turn: the same procedure, four times -------------------
+0.01  script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=1 crt=1 idp=1 kb=KB1
+5     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=2 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
+5     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=3 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2
+5     script ipl-one-gpc.script gpc=4 crt=2 idp=2 kb=KB2

# --- the NBAT for memory configuration 3, then OPS 301 -----------------------
+11    keys ITEM 1 + 3 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 2 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 3 + 2 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 4 + 3 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 5 + 4 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 7 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 8 + 2 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 9 + 3 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 0 + 4 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 1 + 0 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 2 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 3 + 2 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 4 + 3 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 8 + 1 EXEC
+8     keys ITEM 1 9 + 2 EXEC
+21    keys OPS 3 0 1 PRO

# --- and only now the third display, with GPC3 already named its commander ---
+20    idppower 3 on
+2     majfunc 3 gnc
+2     idpload 3

# --- AND NOW THE FAULT.  ----------------------------------------------------
#
# This script exists to test the OTHER half of what synchronisation is for.
# Every other run asks whether a healthy computer is ever failed by mistake;
# this one asks whether a computer that genuinely stops IS failed, which is
# the property that makes the CAM and SPEC 6 worth believing.  A set that
# never votes is as wrong as one that votes at random -- it just fails
# quietly, and a pilot would never know a machine had died.
#
# Sixty seconds of settled four-computer running first, so the vote cannot be
# confused with the OPS transition, then GPC 3 is halted at the panel exactly
# as the crew would.  What should follow: the other three fail it within a
# fraction of a second, the CAM lights column 3, and SPEC 6 shows it down.
+60    subtitle Now HALT GPC 3.  The set should notice, and vote it out.
+0     gpc 3
+2     mode HALT
+30    subtitle GPC 3 is halted.  The remaining three should still be in sync.
